    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>For centuries, the wood fence has been the classic boundary for homes. From the white picket fence of American suburbs to close-boarded timber fences around British back gardens, timber is still one of the most widely used fencing materials. A fence is a significant investment, though, and it shapes your garden's privacy, security and kerb appeal for years.</p>
<p>Understanding timber, its natural character, its weak points and its undeniable charm, is worth doing before you dig the first post hole. This guide sets out the honest pros and cons of a timber fence to help you decide whether it is right for your home.</p>

<h2 id="aesthetics">1. Aesthetics</h2>
<p>The main reason homeowners choose wood is its versatility. Unlike vinyl or chain-link, which can look manufactured or industrial, timber has an organic warmth that sits comfortably with planting. It also suits almost any style of house. In the UK, the common styles are:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Closeboard (featheredge):</strong> overlapping vertical boards nailed to horizontal rails between posts, solid and sturdy.</li>
<li><strong>Panel fencing:</strong> ready-made panels slotted or fixed between posts, quick to install and easy to replace.</li>
<li><strong>Hit-and-miss:</strong> boards fixed alternately on each side of the rails, so the fence looks good from both sides and lets some air through.</li>
<li><strong>Slatted:</strong> horizontal slats with narrow gaps, a clean, contemporary look for modern homes.</li>
</ul>
<p>A traditional cottage might suit a spaced picket fence at the front, while a modern home often looks best with slim horizontal slats.</p>
<p>Wood is also one of the few fencing materials whose colour you can change. With stains and paints you can refresh the look of your boundary over the years: a clear finish that shows the grain, a tinted stain in a warm or weathered tone, or an opaque fence paint that picks up the colour of your window frames or front door.</p>

<h2 id="cost-effectiveness">2. Cost-effectiveness</h2>
<p>In upfront cost, timber sits in a useful middle ground. It is usually more affordable than metal railings, composite fencing or premium vinyl. For homeowners enclosing a long boundary on a budget, pressure-treated softwood is often the most economical option that still gives a solid barrier.</p>
<p>Repairs are cost-effective too. With some interlocking vinyl systems, a damaged section can mean replacing whole panels, which may be hard to colour-match. With a timber fence you can usually replace individual boards, rails or a single panel. That modular nature means minor damage rarely calls for a complete rebuild. For a detailed look at what affects the price, see our guide to <a href="/blog/privacy-fence-cost">privacy fence cost</a>.</p>

<h2 id="customisation">3. Customisation</h2>
<p>Wood is one of the easiest fencing materials to work. It can be cut, shaped and joined in endless ways, which helps on sloping or uneven gardens. There are two ways to deal with a slope:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stepping:</strong> each section stays level and the fence drops in steps down the slope. Panel fences in the UK are usually stepped, with gravel boards used to fill the triangular gaps underneath.</li>
<li><strong>Following the slope:</strong> a fence built on site, such as closeboard or featheredge, can have its rails set parallel to the ground so the fence follows the slope smoothly, leaving no large gaps for pets to squeeze under.</li>
</ul>
<p>The decorative options are wide too: flat, pointed or rounded board tops, trellis toppers for climbing plants, decorative post caps, and sturdy gates with good-quality ironmongery. You can even add an arch or pergola over the entrance. That flexibility lets the fence reflect your own style rather than being a one-size-fits-all product.</p>

<h2 id="privacy">4. Privacy</h2>
<p>For a secluded garden, a solid timber fence is hard to beat. Closeboard or tightly fitted panels form a visual and physical barrier. Unlike chain-link, which gives no privacy at all, thick boards provide a dense screen.</p>
<p>A solid timber fence can also soften some noise, such as a neighbour's lawnmower or distant traffic. It will not block the sound of a busy road, and dense, solid fences without gaps do this best. If privacy is your main aim, check the height limits before you buy: in England a fence is generally permitted development if it is no more than 2 m (6 ft 7 in) high, or 1 m (3 ft 3 in) next to a highway used by vehicles. Listed buildings, conservation areas and properties with planning conditions may differ, so check with your local planning authority. Our guide to <a href="/blog/privacy-fence-mistakes">privacy fence mistakes</a> covers boundaries and planning in more detail.</p>

<!--figure:tall-wooden-privacy-fence-snow|Close-boarded timber gives full privacy, even in winter when hedges are bare.-->

<h2 id="maintenance-needs">5. Maintenance needs</h2>
<p>The biggest drawback of a wood fence is the upkeep. Timber is exposed to the weather all year. UV light breaks down the surface of the wood and turns it silver-grey over time, and repeated wetting and drying can lead to swelling, warping, twisting or splitting.</p>
<p>To keep a timber fence looking good and structurally sound, plan for regular care:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Clean it</strong> with a stiff brush and a suitable fence or timber cleaner. If you use a pressure washer, choose a low setting and keep the nozzle well back, as too much pressure can raise the grain and damage the boards.</li>
<li><strong>Re-apply a stain, preservative or paint</strong> every few years, as the product label advises. How often depends on the timber, the treatment, your climate and how exposed the fence is.</li>
<li><strong>Check the posts and fixings</strong> once a year, especially after winter storms, and replace loose nails or screws before boards start to move.</li>
</ul>
<p>How long a timber fence lasts depends on the timber, the treatment, the quality of installation, your climate and how well it is maintained. A neglected fence will deteriorate far sooner than one that is looked after. For some households, this ongoing work, or the cost of paying someone to do it, is the deciding factor against wood.</p>

<!--figure:weathered-peeling-fence-boards|Without regular treatment, the finish fades and peels and the timber starts to weather.-->

<h2 id="durability-issues">6. Durability issues</h2>
<p>Even with good care, timber is vulnerable to decay. The main threat to a fence is wet rot, a general term for fungal decay that develops in timber that stays damp. (In the UK, "dry rot" refers to a specific fungus, <em>Serpula lacrymans</em>, which is mostly found indoors in buildings rather than in garden fences.) The posts and the bottom of the boards are most at risk, because that is where the timber meets wet soil.</p>
<p>You can reduce the risk considerably:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Use the right posts.</strong> Choose timber posts treated for ground contact (often labelled Use Class 4, or UC4), or use concrete posts, which are common in the UK and do not rot.</li>
<li><strong>Keep timber off the soil.</strong> Gravel boards, made of concrete or treated timber, sit along the bottom of the fence and take the wet so the panels or boards do not. They can be replaced on their own.</li>
<li><strong>Help water drain away.</strong> Shape concrete around post bases so water runs off, and avoid piling soil, leaves or bark mulch against the fence.</li>
</ul>
<p>Insects are a smaller concern in most UK gardens. In parts of the US, termites and carpenter ants can attack wooden fences. In the UK the main insect pests are wood-boring beetles, which are less commonly a problem in treated fence timber. Rot is usually the bigger issue.</p>
<p>If you buy pressure-treated timber, check whether the supplier or treatment manufacturer offers a guarantee and what conditions apply, such as ground contact or re-treating cut ends with an end-grain preservative.</p>

<!--figure:weathered-wooden-rail-fence|Damp, moss and ground contact are what shorten a wooden fence’s life.-->

<h2 id="environmental-impact">7. Environmental impact</h2>
<p>Timber is a renewable material, and it can be a responsible choice if it comes from well-managed forests. Look for FSC (Forest Stewardship Council) or PEFC certified timber, which is widely available from UK timber merchants. Wood is also biodegradable at the end of its life, unlike plastic-based fencing such as vinyl (PVC).</p>
<p>Most treated fence timber today uses modern preservatives designed for outdoor use. Follow the supplier's handling advice: wear gloves when cutting, avoid breathing in sawdust, and do not burn treated offcuts. If you choose a hardwood, make sure it comes with credible certification, as some tropical hardwoods are not sustainably harvested. Naturally durable timbers such as cedar and larch are often used untreated, which some homeowners prefer.</p>

<h2 id="who-is-it-right-for">8. Who is a wood fence right for?</h2>
<p>Choosing a wood fence often comes down to your lifestyle and your plans for the property.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The DIY enthusiast:</strong> if you enjoy weekend projects and looking after your home, the upkeep of a timber fence will not feel like a chore.</li>
<li><strong>The budget-conscious homeowner:</strong> if you need to enclose the garden for a new dog or a toddler and want to keep the upfront cost down, timber offers solid protection without a premium price.</li>
<li><strong>The natural-materials purist:</strong> if your home is a period property or has a rustic, cottage or farmhouse character, synthetic fencing can look out of place, and timber keeps the style intact.</li>
<li><strong>Homeowners who want flexibility:</strong> if you like the idea of changing colour, adding trellis or replacing a single panel rather than a whole run, timber gives you that freedom.</li>
</ul>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Whatever timber you choose, set it on concrete posts or ground-contact treated posts with gravel boards along the bottom. Keeping the timber out of the soil is one of the simplest ways to help a fence last.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>How long does a typical wood fence last?</h3>
<p>It depends on the timber, the treatment, the installation, your climate and how well the fence is maintained. As a general comparison, naturally durable timbers such as cedar and larch, and well-treated softwood on concrete or ground-contact posts, tend to outlast cheap untreated timber. Check any guarantee offered by the supplier or treatment manufacturer for its stated term and conditions.</p>
<h3>Is it better to stain or paint a wood fence?</h3>
<p>Many people prefer a stain or a fence treatment. These soak into the timber, let the grain show and tend to wear gradually, so recoating is simpler. Paint sits on the surface and gives a bold, solid colour, but some paints can crack or peel as the timber moves, which means more preparation later. Microporous exterior paints designed for timber can reduce this. Whichever you choose, follow the manufacturer's instructions on preparation and drying times.</p>
<h3>Does a wood fence increase property value?</h3>
<p>A well-kept fence can add appeal for buyers, because privacy and a secure garden matter to many households, but any effect on value depends on your local market and the fence's condition. A rotten or leaning fence can put buyers off, as it signals a job they will need to pay for.</p>
<h3>Which wood is the most rot-resistant?</h3>
<p>Among common fencing timbers, cedar and larch are naturally more resistant to decay than untreated softwood, because of the natural compounds in the wood. On a budget, pressure-treated softwood is impregnated with preservative to resist rot and insects; for posts, choose timber treated for ground contact (UC4) or use concrete posts.</p>
<h3>When is the best time of year to install a wood fence?</h3>
<p>Fences can be installed most of the year. Avoid frozen or waterlogged ground, which makes digging and setting posts difficult. Many people find the dormant season, from late autumn to early spring, convenient because planting along the boundary is cut back and less likely to be damaged, but a dry spell in any season works well.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>A wood fence is more than a boundary; it is part of your garden. It offers warmth, privacy and flexibility that synthetic materials find hard to match, and it is easy to repair. In return it asks for regular care, and how long it lasts depends on the timber, the treatment, the installation and the upkeep. Choose durable or properly treated timber, keep it off the soil and stay on top of maintenance, and you can enjoy an attractive, practical fence for many years. Before you start, read our guides to <a href="/blog/privacy-fence-mistakes">privacy fence mistakes</a> and <a href="/blog/privacy-fence-cost">privacy fence cost</a>, or see how timber performs underfoot in <a href="/blog/wood-deck-pros-and-cons">wood deck pros and cons</a>. For help designing your garden, explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
